###### Abstract

The globular cluster M15 has recently been found to host a possible central black hole with a mass of \\sim2000 M_{\\odot}. A deep, high-resolution _Chandra_ image failed to detect the "nucleus" of the cluster in X-rays. The upper limit on the X-ray luminosity (L_{\\rm x}\\lesssim 5.6\\times 10^{32} erg s{}^{-1}) corresponds to a bolometric Eddington ratio of L_{\\rm bol}/L_{\\rm Edd}\\lesssim(2-4)\\times 10^{-8}. Combining this limit with an estimate of the electron density of the intracluster ionized plasma derived from pulsar dispersion measures, we show that the radiative efficiency of the accretion flow, if it accretes at the Bondi rate, must be much lower than that of a standard optically thick, geometrically thin disk. black hole physics -- globular clusters: individual (M15) -- X-rays: general +

